# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# opt-rt: fully preemptible (PREEMPT_RT) kernel.
#
# For the layers where a deadline miss is the failure, not a slowdown: telco
# vRAN/NFV hosts, HFT execution nodes, and anything with a sub-millisecond
# response contract.
#
# The trade is real and goes the wrong way for a general cloud fleet:
# PREEMPT_RT converts most spinlocks into sleeping locks and threads the
# interrupt handlers, which buys bounded worst-case latency at a measurable
# cost in total throughput.  On a machine sold by the vCPU-hour, that cost is
# revenue.  So this is opt-in per fleet, never the default.
#
# It also replaces the preemption model outright: PREEMPT_DYNAMIC and
# PREEMPT_LAZY are unavailable under RT, so `preempt=` no longer tunes
# anything at boot -- the decision moves back into the image.
#
#   make PROFILE=hypervisor KVMHOST_EXTRA=opt-rt build
#
# Pair with isolcpus/nohz_full/rcu_nocbs for the RT cores (docs/TUNING.md) and
# with KVM's own RT settings for vCPU threads; RT without CPU isolation mostly
# buys the throughput loss and not the determinism.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
CONFIG_PREEMPT_RT=y
# CONFIG_PREEMPT_LAZY is not set
# CONFIG_PREEMPT_DYNAMIC is not set

# RT excludes transparent hugepages and NUMA balancing upstream -- both do
# unbounded work at fault time (compaction, page migration), which is exactly
# what a deadline cannot absorb.
#
# Consequence for a hypervisor, and it is not a small one: with THP gone,
# guest memory has to come from *preallocated* hugetlbfs, sized at boot.  You
# lose memory overcommit and the ability to grow a guest's backing on demand.
# An RT/NFV host is configured that way regardless -- static 1G pages, pinned
# vCPUs -- so this codifies the deployment rather than constraining it.
# CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE is not set
# CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_MADVISE is not set
# CONFIG_NUMA_BALANCING is not set
